// src/v4/std/diagnostic.dag
//
// Scope: fail-closed Diagnostic schema with structural reason + at +
//        correction. Per STRUCTURE.md architectural commitment #3 the
//        schema CARRIES the suggested-correction obligation — the
//        substrate must be able to enforce THESIS:103-105 "show the
//        correct code", so the field is part of the type, not an
//        optional bolt-on.
// Anchor: THESIS.md "Show the correct code" §103-105 + INVARIANTS P3
//
// Owns:
//   - Diagnostic { reason: Symbol, at: Locus, correction: Correction }
//   - Outcome<T> = Produced { value: T } | Rejected { diagnostic: Diagnostic }
//     (DECISIONS.md **I** 2026-05-16 — fail-closed success|failure carrier for
//     pipeline stages and verification; co-located with Diagnostic.)
//   - Locus = Textual | NodeLocus | PortLocus  (WHERE a diagnostic
//     points; a `Textual` locus carries a file identity AND an Extent,
//     so "a span in a specific file" is ONE boundary fact — P2 facts-
//     flow-forward — not two mutually-exclusive variants)
//   - Extent = WholeFile | ByteRange  (the optional narrowing of a
//     textual locus: a whole file, or a byte range within it)
//   - Correction = Suggested(Node) | Unavailable(NoCorrectionReason)
//     (typed sum — NOT Option<Node>. "no correction available" is a
//     structural Unavailable case carrying a NoCorrectionReason, never
//     a bare None enforced by convention. Type-enforced per INVARIANTS
//     P2, not API-enforced.)
//     The suggested correction is a Node — the bounded kernel says Node
//     is the ONLY recursive type, so a subtree of Nodes IS a Node (no
//     separate "NodeFragment": that would be a decorative alias the
//     compiler sees through, per feedback_nodes_are_nodes +
//     feedback_naming_is_aliasing). WHERE the correction applies is not
//     carried by the fragment — it is the Diagnostic's own `at: Locus`.
//   - NoCorrectionReason = UserInputBoundary | AmbiguousIntent
//     | ExternalContractUnknown   (closed — the exhaustive partition of
//     "why can't the fix be mechanically derived": intent the compiler
//     cannot know (one missing user choice = UserInputBoundary; many
//     valid candidates = AmbiguousIntent) OR information the compiler
//     cannot see (behind an external contract = ExternalContractUnknown).
//     NO "..." — a 4th reason is a substrate-extension STOP, not a
//     silent escape. Every "no fix" thus answers WHY structurally.)
//
// `reason` is a NAME-REFERENCE, not a closed enum. A diagnostic reason
// is owned by the DIMENSION that raises it — the compiler core owns its
// primitive reasons, each lens owns its own, a user-defined dimension
// owns its own (THESIS §1.5). diagnostic.dag owns the SCHEMA, never a
// central reason set: a central closed enum would force every lens /
// user dimension to edit THIS file to add its failure vocabulary,
// violating P2 single-authority and foreclosing user-defined
// dimensions. `reason: Symbol` is the node.dag cross-declaration idiom
// (a name-reference resolved through the namespace — inhabitance, not a
// readable String the kernel branches on; the Symbol is K-1-opaque).
// The reason set thus PROJECTS over the known dimensions; it is not
// enumerated here.
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: Node (the `Suggested` correction + the `NodeLocus`
//     payload). Conj/Disj are the record/sum surface sugar.
//   (Symbol is substrate-ambient per std/node.dag K-1; Int is kernel-
//   ambient — neither is imported.)
//
// Status: T-3 modeled 2026-05-15 (smart-eagle-570); `reason` + `Locus`
// revised per PR #3161 review (three BLOCKING findings — see modeling
// notes). This header is the I/O contract downstream workers build to;
// revising it is a modeling decision surfaced in review, not a
// unilateral worker edit.


module v4.std.diagnostic


import v4.std.node { Node }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// SCHEMA FILE. diagnostic.dag declares the fail-closed `Diagnostic`
// type and its structural carriers — it owns no producer logic. The
// pipeline stages (T-6 tokenize … T-22 eval) and the lens reads
// CONSTRUCT `Diagnostic` values; this file is the shape they construct
// to (INVARIANTS P3 — every failure path is a typed Diagnostic, never a
// panic, never a silent `None`).
//
// REVISED PER PR #3161 REVIEW. The first cut modeled `reason` as one
// central closed `NamedReason` enum and `Locus` as four flat variants.
// Three BLOCKING review findings corrected that:
//   - A central closed reason enum forces every lens / user-defined
//     dimension to edit THIS file to add its failure vocabulary —
//     P2 single-authority violation + forecloses THESIS §1.5 user-
//     defined dimensions. Fix: `reason` is a name-reference (`Symbol`)
//     projecting over the dimensions; the reason set is NOT enumerated
//     here. (v3 had already split this two-layer; v3/std/diagnostics.dag
//     `CompilerDiagnosticKind` + `LensInstanceKindWitness` — v4 models
//     it fresh as a single name-reference, no layering carrier.)
//   - `FileLocus` and `SpanLocus` as mutually-exclusive variants meant
//     "a span in a specific file" could not be carried as one fact.
//     Fix: `Textual { file, extent }` composes file identity with the
//     extent; a file is not a special case — it is a `Textual` locus
//     whose `extent` is `WholeFile`.
//   - The raw `Int` span pair admits illegal states (negative offset,
//     start > end). It is isolated into `Extent.ByteRange` and that
//     coproduct carries the 🟡 scaffold classification + named trigger.
//
// NO MESSAGE STRING. A `Diagnostic` carries `reason` (a name-reference),
// not a free-text `message: String`. The reason resolves to a structural
// declaration owned by its dimension; human-readable rendering is a
// boundary concern (the std/node.dag K-1 discipline — an opaque
// identity's spelling stays out of kernel/lens logic).
//
// Every coproduct below carries the full Practice-4 🟢/🟡/🔴
// classification + the 5-pattern dissolution ledger (modeling-
// discipline.md §4 — operator-ratified binding 2026-05-15; the doc
// enumerates FIVE patterns, the fifth being Parameterized-family).
// `reason` is a name-reference, not a coproduct — no ledger applies; the
// `Diagnostic` record is a Conj, likewise none. `Outcome<T>` (declared
// after `Diagnostic` in this file) is a coproduct and carries its own
// ledger there.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Extent — the narrowing of a textual locus. A textual diagnostic
// points either at a whole file or at a byte range within it.
//
//   - WholeFile — no narrowing; the locus is the file as a whole.
//   - ByteRange — a half-open `[start, end)` source-offset range.
//     SCAFFOLD — the 🟡 driver (see ledger). With only the kernel-
//     ambient `Int` available at T-3, `{ start: Int, end: Int }`
//     STRUCTURALLY ADMITS illegal states — a negative offset, or
//     `start > end` — which modeling-discipline.md §2 (illegal states
//     unrepresentable) forbids; no bounded-offset substrate exists yet
//     to exclude them. It is a TRACKED scaffold, declared with the
//     three bridge properties:
//       (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the carrier is provisional, not
//           the terminal byte-range shape.
//       (b) bounds on use — a producer MUST construct only a valid
//           half-open range (`0 <= start <= end`). Until the substrate
//           enforces it the invariant is producer-side; a ByteRange
//           violating it is a producer bug, not a legal value.
//       (c) dissolution trigger — when std/cardinality.dag's refinement
//           substrate lands (its own T-3 entry — cardinality refinement
//           + bounded iteration), `start`/`end` become refined non-
//           negative offsets with `start <= end` type-enforced, and
//           this scaffold dissolves.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟡 YELLOW (scaffold). The SUM
// itself is terminal (whole-file vs byte-range are genuine, exclusive
// alternatives — see patterns); what makes it YELLOW is the `ByteRange`
// payload: a richer source (a refined bounded-offset pair) exists but
// its substrate is not ready.
//   Named trigger: std/cardinality.dag's refinement substrate lands ⇒
//   `ByteRange`'s `{ start, end }` refine to non-negative, `start <=
//   end` offsets; the coproduct then reclassifies 🟢 GREEN.
// Five patterns attempted on the SUM (all FAIL to dissolve it):
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: an extent is consumed uniformly by the
//      diagnostic renderer; not a per-consumer fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: `WholeFile` (nullary) and `ByteRange`
//      ({start, end}) have DIFFERENT shapes; promoting to a flat
//      `{ whole: Bool, start?, end? }` record makes "whole-file with a
//      start offset" representable — the inverse of P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: an extent is not an algebra carrier; no
//      richer std/ structure it traces to.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an extent is EITHER whole-file OR a byte
//      range — mutually-exclusive alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two variants are not `F<X>`
//      for X over a declared set; not a mechanical copy of any set
//      (not a Practice-7 projection).
type Extent
  = WholeFile
  | ByteRange { start: Int, end: Int }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Locus — WHERE a Diagnostic points. A Diagnostic carries exactly one
// `Locus` (`at`). Three genuine kinds of location:
//
//   - Textual carries a `file` identity (an opaque `Symbol` per K-1 —
//     a file path is an identifier, never a readable `String` the
//     kernel branches on) AND an `Extent`. A file is NOT a special
//     case: a whole-file diagnostic is a `Textual` locus whose
//     `extent` is `WholeFile`; a finer one narrows with `ByteRange`.
//     File identity and byte extent travel as ONE fact (P2 facts-flow-
//     forward) — the fix for the review finding that split them.
//   - NodeLocus carries the offending `Node` itself — a structural
//     position in the parsed graph. `Node` well-formedness is
//     std/node.dag's `node_well_formed` concern, not a Locus sub-state.
//   - PortLocus carries an opaque `Symbol` naming a pipeline-stage
//     port.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). The residual
// T-3 scaffold (raw byte offsets) is isolated inside `Extent`, which
// carries its own 🟡 ledger; `Locus`'s own three-way structure is
// terminal. Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the locus is consumed uniformly by the
//      diagnostic renderer; it is not a per-consumer fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the three variants carry DIFFERENT
//      payload shapes (a `{ file, extent }` record; a `Node`; a
//      `Symbol`). Collapsing to a flat `{ kind, file?, extent?, node?,
//      port? }` record makes illegal combinations representable (a
//      `NodeLocus` carrying a file) — the inverse of P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a source locus is not an algebra carrier;
//      there is no richer std/ structure the SUM traces to.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a Diagnostic points at EXACTLY ONE kind of
//      location — textual OR node OR port; alternatives, not
//      simultaneous coordinates. (Within `Textual`, file + extent ARE
//      composed — that composition is the record, already done; it is
//      not a further coproduct axis.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three variants are not `F<X>`
//      for X over a declared set; their payloads are structurally
//      distinct (a record / a `Node` / a `Symbol`), not a mechanical
//      copy of an enumerable set (not a Practice-7 projection).
// Terminal: an irreducible set of source-location kinds at the
// diagnostic boundary.
type Locus
  = Textual { file: Symbol, extent: Extent }
  | NodeLocus { node: Node }
  | PortLocus { port: Symbol }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NoCorrectionReason — the exhaustive partition of WHY a fix cannot be
// mechanically derived. Carried by `Correction.Unavailable` so that "no
// fix" is itself a structural answer, never a bare absence.
//
// Two root causes, three cells (header):
//   - intent the compiler cannot KNOW:
//       UserInputBoundary — one missing user choice; the fix is a
//         single decision only the author can make.
//       AmbiguousIntent — many valid candidate fixes; the compiler
//         will not pick one (picking = fabrication, the no-engine STOP).
//   - information the compiler cannot SEE:
//       ExternalContractUnknown — the fix lies behind an external
//         contract (an extdeps boundary) the compiler cannot inspect.
//
// CLOSED — no "…". A fourth "why no fix" is a substrate-extension STOP
// (header), an edit to this declaration routed to operator ratification.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: consumed uniformly by the renderer that
//      explains why no fix is offered; not a per-consumer fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the only "data" form is a `String`
//      reason — the opaque-string STOP again; the closed three-way
//      partition is the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: not an algebra carrier; the variants are
//      the partition of an epistemic boundary, with no richer std/
//      source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a "no fix" answers EXACTLY ONE why — the
//      two root causes (can't-know / can't-see) are disjoint and the
//      three cells mutually exclusive; never simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three variants are not `F<X>`
//      over a separately-declared set; they are an epistemic-boundary
//      partition, not a mechanical copy of some enumerable set (not a
//      Practice-7 projection).
// Terminal: the exhaustive, irreducible partition of "why can't the fix
// be mechanically derived" — every no-fix case answers structurally.
type NoCorrectionReason
  = UserInputBoundary
  | AmbiguousIntent
  | ExternalContractUnknown


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Correction — the structural answer to "show the correct code"
// (THESIS §103-105, STRUCTURE.md architectural commitment #3). Every
// Diagnostic carries one; the schema makes the fix obligation type-
// enforced, not a convention.
//
//   - Suggested carries the fix as a `Node` — the bounded kernel's one
//     recursive type, so a subtree of Nodes IS a Node; there is no
//     separate `NodeFragment` alias (the header's nodes-are-nodes
//     point). WHERE the fix applies is NOT carried here — it is the
//     Diagnostic's own `at: Locus`.
//   - Unavailable carries a `NoCorrectionReason`. "No fix" is a
//     structural case that answers WHY, never a bare `None` — this is
//     the brief's explicit STOP ("Option<Node>") and INVARIANTS P2
//     (type-enforced, not API-enforced).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Diagnostic consumer that surfaces
//      the fix handles BOTH arms (render the suggested `Node`, or render
//      the no-fix reason); it is not a per-consumer fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the arms carry DIFFERENT shapes (a
//      `Node`; a `NoCorrectionReason`). Collapsing to `{ has_fix: Bool,
//      node?, reason? }` ratifies exactly the bare-`None` illegal state
//      the header forbids — `has_fix: true` with no `node` becomes
//      representable (the inverse of P2).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a correction is not an algebra carrier;
//      there is no richer std/ structure it traces to.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a Diagnostic either HAS a mechanically-
//      derivable fix OR does not — disjoint alternatives, never
//      simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two arms are not `F<X>` over a
//      declared set; `Suggested` and `Unavailable` carry structurally
//      distinct payloads (a `Node`; a `NoCorrectionReason`), not a
//      mechanical copy of an enumerable set (not a Practice-7
//      projection).
// Terminal: the irreducible two-case answer to the "show the correct
// code" obligation — a typed sum, never `Option<Node>`.
type Correction
  = Suggested { node: Node }
  | Unavailable { reason: NoCorrectionReason }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Diagnostic — the fail-closed typed error (INVARIANTS P3). Every
// failure path in the pipeline and every empty lens read surfaces as a
// `Diagnostic` value; there is no panic and no silent `None`.
//
// Three structural fields, no free-text message (modeling notes):
//   reason      — WHY it fired. A NAME-REFERENCE (opaque `Symbol`, K-1)
//                 resolved through the namespace to a reason DECLARED
//                 and OWNED by the dimension that raised it (the
//                 compiler core, a lens, or a user-defined dimension).
//                 diagnostic.dag owns the schema, not the reason set —
//                 so adding a dimension never edits this file (P2
//                 single-authority; THESIS §1.5 user-defined
//                 dimensions). This is std/node.dag's cross-declaration
//                 idiom (inhabitance via name-reference), NOT a
//                 free-text `String` — the Symbol is kernel-opaque and
//                 resolved structurally.
//   at          — WHERE it points (a `Locus`).
//   correction  — the "show the correct code" answer (a `Correction`).
//
// This is a record (Conj), not a coproduct — no Practice-4 ledger.
type Diagnostic {
  reason: Symbol
  at: Locus
  correction: Correction
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Outcome<T> — ratified as v4/DECISIONS.md **I** (2026-05-16). The shared
// fail-closed carrier for "produced value vs rejected with Diagnostic"
// (`TASKS.md` / `STRUCTURE.md` `Result<…, Diagnostic>` **prose** names this
// same surface — the `.dag` authority is `Outcome<…>` here; no parallel
// `Result<ok, err>` type in `std/`; see **I** for M6/P2 reconciliation.
// variant names `Produced` / `Rejected` and field names `value` /
// `diagnostic` are binding for downstream: verification.dag TestClaim
// expectations, Tier-2 numeric carriers, collection partial-access, etc.)
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — five patterns attempted; classification: 🟢 GREEN
// (terminal). Ledger:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every consumer of an `Outcome<T>` handles
//      both arms uniformly at the stage boundary (carry success forward
//      or surface the `Diagnostic`); not a per-site fact to scatter.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: `Produced` carries `T` and `Rejected` carries
//      a full `Diagnostic` — different shapes. Collapsing to `{ ok: Bool,
//      value?, diagnostic? }` ratifies illegal mutual states (success
//      with no value, rejection with no diagnostic) — the inverse of P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: an outcome is not an algebra carrier; neither
//      arm traces to a richer single `std/` operation family to factor into.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: success and failure are mutually exclusive
//      alternatives at one boundary — not simultaneous coordinates in a
//      product of independent dimensions.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two variants are not mechanically
//      the same payload shape `F<X>` for `X` over a separately-declared
//      enumerable set (Practice 7). `Produced`/`Rejected` are the
//      irreducible partition of produced vs fail-closed reject — not an
//      enumerated copy of such a set.
// Terminal: the irreducible typed sum for fail-closed stage returns beside
// `Diagnostic` — never a bare optional on the error path (INVARIANTS P3).
type Outcome<T>
  = Produced { value: T }
  | Rejected { diagnostic: Diagnostic }
